{
  "title": "How innovative industrial clusters affect entrepreneurship in Chinese cities",
  "description": "Urban entrepreneurial activity is highly uneven across space, making place-based industrial policy central to sustainable urban development. This study exploits the phased rollout of China's innovative industrial cluster policy as a quasi-natural experiment and uses new-firm registration data for 284 cities from 2009 to 2024. The manuscript reports that the policy increased urban entrepreneurial vitality in pilot cities relative to non-pilot cities through resource agglomeration, easing financing constraints and expanding market demand, and generated spatial spillovers that peaked near 100 km and continued to about 200 km.",
